hadiths[8]{hadithnumber,text}:
"956","Ahmad bin Abdah narrated to us, Abd al-Warith narrated to us, meaning Ibn Sa’id, on the authority of Yahya, H. Ya’qub bin Ibrahim narrated to us, Ibn Aliyah, on the authority of Yahya bin Abi Ishaq, on the authority of Amr bin Ali, on the authority of Yazid bin Zurai’, and Bishr bin al-Mufaddal, saying: Yahya told us Bin Abi Ishaq, narrated by Al-San’ani, narrated by Bishr bin Al-Mufaddal, narrated by Yahya, he said: I asked Anas bin Malik about shortening the prayer, and he said: “ We traveled with the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, from Medina to Mecca, praying two rak’ahs until we returned. I asked him, “Did he stay in Mecca?” He said: Yes, he stayed there ten times.” This is the hadith of Al-Dourqi. Ahmed bin Abdah said: He used to lead us in two rak’ahs of prayer. Ahmad and Amr bin Ali said: On the authority of Anas, he said: We went out with the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, and they did not say: I asked Anas. Abu Bakr said: I do not remember anything from the narrations of the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, that he intended to stay for several days in some of his travels. There is no information other than this trip in which he came to Mecca for the Farewell Pilgrimage, for he made it intending to perform Hajj, so he came to Mecca on the fourth morning. It has passed since Dhul-Hijjah"
"957","Likewise, Bundar told us, Muhammad bin Bakr told us, Ibn Jurayj told us, on the authority of Ata’, he said: Jabir bin Abdullah said: “The Messenger of God came May God’s prayers and peace be upon him on the fourth morning of Dhul-Hijjah. From Dhul-Hijjah, he stayed in Mecca for four days, excluding the time during which he was traveling from the fourth beginning, until he arrived there and some day The fifth was intending to do this residency when he came to Mecca, so he stayed in residence for the rest of the fourth, fifth, sixth, seventh, and eighth for some time. day, which is the day of the Tarwiyah, then he left Mecca on the day of the Tarwiyah and prayed the noon prayer in Mina."
"958","Likewise, Abu Musa narrated to us, Ishaq Al-Azraq narrated to us, Sufyan Al-Thawri narrated to us, on the authority of Abd al-Aziz bin Rafi’, he said: I asked Anas bin Malik, I said: Tell me something. I learned it from the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace. Where did he pray the noon prayer on the day of al-Tarwiyah? He said: In Mina. Abu Bakr said: I said: So he, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, stayed for the rest of the day of Tarwiyah in Mina, the night of Arafat, and then the morning of Arafat. So he walked to the parking place in Arafat, combining noon and afternoon prayers there, then he walked to the parking place, and stood at the parking place until the sun set, then he pushed until He returned to Muzdalifah, and combined the Maghrib and Isha prayers in Muzdalifah and spent the night there until morning, then he prayed the morning prayer in Muzdalifah, and walked He returned to Mina, and stayed in Mina for the rest of the Day of Sacrifice, two days of the days of Tashreeq, and some of the third day of Tashreeq. On the sun, on the days of Tashreeq, he stoned the three stones, returned to Mecca, and prayed the noon and afternoon prayers on the last days of Tashreeq, then the sunset and evening prayers. Then he lay down in Al-Muhassab, and this was the completion of all ten days that he spent in Mecca and Mina on both occasions and in Arafat. So Anas bin Malik made All of this is a stay in Mecca, and neither Mina nor Arafat is from Mecca. Rather, they are outside of Mecca and Arafat is outside of the Haram as well, so how can this be? Is he leaving the Sacred Mosque from Mecca? The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said when he mentioned Mecca and its prohibition: “Indeed, God made Mecca sacred on the day He created it.” The heavens and the earth, for it is inviolable according to the prohibition of God until the Day of Resurrection. Its game will not be taken away, its trees will not be uprooted, and its clearings will not be deserted.” If it had been Arafat From Mecca, it is not permissible for game to be hunted at Arafat, and no trees should be supported over it, nor should a desert place be kept there, and the people of prayer are unanimously agreed that Arafat is outside. Of the Haram, it is clear and proven that it is not from Mecca, and if the name Mecca applies to all of the Haram, then Arafat comes out of Mecca because it It comes out of the Haram and Mina is clear from the construction and development of Mecca. It may be possible that the name Mecca applies to the entire Haram, so Mina is inside... The Holy Mosque, and I think the news of Aisha indicates that what was behind the building connected to each other is not from Mecca, and so is the news of Ibn Omar. As for the news of Aisha"
"959","Abu Musa and Abdul Jabbar said: Sufyan told us, on the authority of Hisham bin Urwa, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Aisha, that the Prophet prayed May God bless him and grant him peace: “When he enters Mecca, he enters it from its top, and exits from its bottom.” This is the wording of the hadith of Abu Musa"
"960","Abu Kurayb told us, Abu Usama told us, on the authority of Hisham bin Urwa, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Aisha, that the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace “He entered in the year of the conquest from Kadha from the top of Mecca.” Hisham said: My father used to enter from both of them, and my father used to enter the most from Kadha."
"961","As for the hadith of Ibn Umar, Bandara told us, he said: Yahya told us, in the hand of God, Nafi’ told me, on the authority of Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of God, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, “entered Mecca from the upper bend at Batha’, and left from the lower bend.” Abu Bakr said: Ibn Umar’s statement: The Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, entered Mecca from the upper “Thani” indicates that the “Al-Thani” is not from Mecca. And the second part of the sanctuary, and behind it also of the sanctuary, and likewise from the sanctuary, and also behind it of the sanctuary to the marks that I knew between the sanctuary. And between the solution, how is it permissible to say: The Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, entered Mecca from Mecca? If the second had been from Mecca and almost from Mecca, it would not have been It is permissible to say: The Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, entered Mecca from Al-Thanya and so on. It may be permissible to argue that the entire Sacred Mosque is from Mecca, based on his saying, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him: “God made Mecca sacred on the day He created the heavens.” And the land.” All of the sanctuaries may have the name Mecca on them, except that what is known among people is that Mecca is the site of continuous construction. One by one, the one who says: So-and-so left from Mecca to Mina, and returned from Mina to Mecca, and if you consider the news of the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, And with regard to rituals, I found something similar to this word many times in the reports. As for Arafat and what is behind the Sacred Mosque, there is no doubt or suspicion that it is not From Mecca, and the evidence that the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, departed from Mina on the third day of the days of Tashreeq"
"962","On the authority of Yunus bin Abdul-Ala Thana, he said: Ibn Wahb told us, Amr bin Al-Harith told me, that Qatada bin Di’amah told him, about Anas told him that the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, prayed the noon, afternoon, sunset, and evening prayers, and he lay down on the pebbles. Then he rode home and walked around it.” Abu Bakr said: Then he, may God bless him and grant him peace, left that night, heading towards Medina."
"963","Narrated to us Bundār, narrated to us Abū Bakr al-Ḥanafī, narrated to us Aflaḥ, who said: I heard al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad narrate from ʿĀʾishah (RA), who mentioned some details of the Prophet’s (ﷺ) Hajj and said:\n\n*\He (ﷺ) then permitted his Companions to depart"," so the people set out. He (ﷺ) passed by the House before the dawn prayer"," performed Tawāf around it"," then left"," mounted his camel"," and departed towards Madīnah.\""*\n\nAbū Bakr (the narrator) added:\n*\""We have not heard any of the scholars of jurisprudence (fuqahāʾ) among the people of knowledge consider that what lies beyond the connected buildings in cities is part of the city’s limits"," even if the land beyond the buildings belongs to the city’s boundaries or its attributed lands. Nor do we know them to differ that whoever exits a city intending a journey"," and exits the connected buildings"," may shorten the prayer—even if the lands beyond the buildings are within the city’s limits. Similarly"," we do not know them to differ that if he returns intending a town and enters some of its lands without entering the buildings"," and is outside the limits of the connected buildings"," he may shorten the prayer until he enters the place of the connected buildings.\""*\n\n*\""Nor do we know them to differ that whoever exits Makkah from its people or one who has taken residence there intending a journey where he may shorten the prayer"," and separates from the dwellings of Makkah—placing all its buildings behind his back—even if he is still within the Ḥaram"," he may shorten the prayer.\""*\n\n*\""Thus"," when the Prophet (ﷺ) arrived in Makkah for his Farewell Hajj"," he departed on the Day of Tarwiyah"," having separated from all the buildings of Makkah and traveled to Minā. Minā is not part of the city of Makkah. From a jurisprudential perspective"," it is not permissible to say that when a person exits a city intending a journey—if he may shorten the prayer upon exiting it—that if he exits the buildings within the city"," he is still within the town"," for if he were within the town"," shortening would not be permissible until he exits it entirely.\""*\n\n*\""The correct position"," according to jurisprudence"," is that the Prophet (ﷺ) did not stay in Makkah during the Farewell Hajj except for three full days and nights—the fifth"," sixth"," and seventh—and part of the fourth day"," excluding its night"," the night of the eighth"," and part of the eighth day. He did not intend to stay four full days and nights in a single city. Thus"," this narration"," when contemplated"," does not contradict the statement of the Ḥijāzī scholars that whoever intends to stay four days must complete the prayer. For their opponents say: ‘Whoever intends to stay ten days in a city and four days outside it in some of its lands—which"," like the distance between Makkah and Minā"," is traversed in two trips rather than one—and a day and night in a third location between Minā and ʿArafāt—may shorten the prayer"," and this is not considered an intention to stay fifteen days"," as they claim that whoever intends to stay fifteen days must complete the prayer.’\""*\n\n**CHAPTER:**"""
